        "role": "user",
        "content": "What is the action potential of a myocyte?",
        "sources": [],
        "confidence_score": null,
        "refused": false,
        "namespace_searched": null
      },
      {
        "message_id": "d8d95f93-760a-4d6f-b26c-14f6a991c31f",
        "timestamp": 1782649732.1039228,
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "The action potential of a myocyte is a complex process that involves five phases: Phase 0, Phase 1, Phase 2, Phase 3, and Phase 4 [Physiology_Levy]. \n\nPhase 0 is the rapid depolarization initiated by the influx of Na+ into the myocyte, resulting in the upstroke of the action potential [Physiology_Levy]. \nPhase 1 is an early partial repolarization due to the efflux of K+ through channels that conduct the transient outward current [Physiology_Levy]. \nPhase 2 is the plateau phase, where there is a balance between the influx of Ca++ through calcium channels and the efflux of K+ through several types of potassium channels [Physiology_Levy]. \nPhase 3 is the final repolarization, initiated when the efflux of K+ exceeds the influx of Ca++ [Physiology_Levy]. \nPhase 4 is the resting potential of the fully repolarized cell, determined by the conductance of the cell membrane to K+ [Physiology_Levy].\n\nThe action potential amplitude is dependent on extracellular [Na+], and when extracellular [Na+] is decreased, the amplitude of the action potential decreases [Physiology_Levy]. \n\nThis information is from educational medical textbooks and should not replace consultation with a licensed medical professional.",
