        "timestamp": 1782663164.964363,
        "role": "user",
        "content": "What is the rate-limiting enzyme of the urea cycle and what is its allosteric activator?",
        "sources": [],
        "confidence_score": null,
        "refused": false,
        "namespace_searched": null
      },
      {
        "message_id": "782959e1-16b1-4ce6-99f8-9fc2541b9f4a",
        "timestamp": 1782663164.9980485,
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "The rate-limiting enzyme of the urea cycle is Carbamoyl Phosphate Synthetase I (CPS I) [Biochemistry_Lippinco]. Its allosteric activator is N-Acetylglutamate (NAG) [Biochemistry_Lippinco]. NAG increases the affinity of CPS I for ATP, thereby activating the enzyme and facilitating the urea cycle [Biochemistry_Lippinco]. NAG is synthesized from acetyl CoA and glutamate by N-acetylglutamate synthase (NAGS), and arginine acts as an activator for this reaction [Biochemistry_Lippinco].\n\nThis information is from educational medical textbooks and should not replace consultation with a licensed medical professional.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "document_name": "Biochemistry_Lippinco",
            "geography_iso": "basic_sciences",
