      },
      {
        "message_id": "09f6e898-f659-4697-8600-c4d0249190bb",
        "timestamp": 1782664395.514107,
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "The facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) passes through the parotid gland without synapsing [Histology_Ross, Anatomy_Gray]. The main stem of the facial nerve usually divides into upper (temporofacial) and lower (cervicofacial) branches within the gland [Anatomy_Gray]. As these branches pass through the substance of the parotid gland, they may branch further or take part in an anastomotic network (the parotid plexus) [Anatomy_Gray]. Five terminal groups of branches of the facial nerve\u2014temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical branches\u2014emerge from the parotid gland [Anatomy_Gray]. \n\nThe facial nerve provides motor innervation to the muscles of facial expression [Anatomy_Gray]. The parotid gland also receives sensory innervation from the auriculotemporal nerve, a branch of the mandibular nerve (V3), which is a division of the trigeminal nerve [Anatomy_Gray]. \n\nThis information is from educational medical textbooks and should not replace consultation with a licensed medical professional.",
